IMPORT prelude

-- Taxation Administration Act 1953 (Cth) -- Part IVD, protection for tax whistleblowers: the provisions a WHISTLEBLOWER
-- meets, who discloses tax misconduct and may then be sued, exposed, harmed or threatened for it, and may claim
-- compensation.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who discloses, or may disclose, information about an entity's tax affairs, and who brings or may
-- bring a claim for what was done to them because of it. Every question is phrased as that person would ask it ("Am I
-- ...?", "Does my disclosure ...?", "Can the court order ...?"), on facts that person could state. The Commissioner,
-- the Tax Practitioners Board, the court and the person who harmed the whistleblower get no seat; their duties and
-- powers appear as what the person may claim or is protected from.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED. Part IVD whole, but for ss 14ZZTA and 14ZZZE: ss 14ZZT (with s 2's definitions of eligible
-- recipient and eligible whistleblower), 14ZZU, 14ZZV, 14ZZW, 14ZZX, 14ZZXA, 14ZZY, 14ZZZ, 14ZZZAA, 14ZZZA, 14ZZZB,
-- 14ZZZC, 14ZZZD. Taxation Administration Regulations 2017 ss 13A (the Inspector-General of Taxation an eligible
-- recipient) and 13B (who else may be told the whistleblower's identity).
--
-- Edition: Act Compilation No. 226, compilation date 27 August 2026, register id C2026C00393, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C1953A00001.txt. Regulations Compilation No. 17, compilation date 27 August 2026,
-- register id F2026C00849, as deposited at
-- ../../../taxation-administration-regulations-2017/registers/source-bundle/F2017L01227.txt. The value of a penalty
-- unit (Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA, "$330 (subject to indexation under subsection (3))") is an INPUT.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation at 2026-09-22: "Taxation Administration Act 1953", and at 2026-10-19:
-- "Taxation Administration Regulations 2017", Commonwealth of Australia, licensed under CC BY 4.0
-- (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Taxation Administration Act 1953 Part IVD -- the tax whistleblower's questions`

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`as an answer` b MEANS IF b THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 14ZZU and 14ZZV -- who is a whistleblower, and whom to tell (only if you have reported, or are thinking of reporting, tax misconduct)`

DECLARE `My connection with the entity` IS ONE OF
    `an officer of it`
    `an employee of it`
    `I supply services or goods to it, paid or unpaid`
    `an employee of someone who supplies services or goods to it, paid or unpaid`
    `an associate of it`
    `the spouse or child of a person in one of those groups`
    `a dependant of a person in one of those groups, or of their spouse`
    `no such connection`

-- s 2: "eligible whistleblower has the meaning given by section 14ZZU". s 14ZZU: "An individual is an eligible
-- whistleblower in relation to an entity (within the meaning of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997) if the individual
-- is, or has been, any of the following" (a) to (h). A definition the text hands to a list (ruling of 2026-11-05 (a)),
-- introduced by "if": JUST FALSE where no head holds. (h) "an individual prescribed by the regulations": the deposited
-- Regulations prescribe none (finding 2), so it is not offered. "Entity" (ITAA 1997 s 960-100) and "associate" (ITAA
-- 1936 s 318) are borrowed terms, asked.
@export Am I an "eligible whistleblower" for the business, company, trust, partnership, fund or person whose tax affairs I am reporting on, so that my disclosures about it can be protected (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZU)? Current and former officers, employees, suppliers (paid or unpaid) and their employees, associates, and the spouses, children and dependants of any of them qualify.
GIVEN `is it an entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you are reporting on is an "entity" for tax purposes: an individual, a company or other body corporate, a partnership, a trust, a superannuation fund, an unincorporated association or a government body (almost anything that has tax affairs counts)
      `what is my connection with it` IS A `My connection with the entity` @desc Your connection with the entity, now or in the past: officer (such as a director or secretary), employee, supplier of services or goods to it (paid or unpaid), employee of such a supplier, associate of it (as the tax law defines associate, for example a relative, partner or controlling shareholder), the spouse or child of any of those, a dependant of any of those or of their spouse, or no such connection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Am I an eligible whistleblower` MEANS
    `is it an entity` AND (NOT (`what is my connection with it` EQUALS `no such connection`))

DECLARE `Kind of entity` IS ONE OF
    `a body corporate, such as a company`
    `a trust`
    `a partnership`
    `another kind of entity`

DECLARE `The person I told` IS ONE OF
    `an auditor of the entity, or a member of an audit team auditing it`
    `a registered tax agent or BAS agent who provides it tax agent or BAS services`
    `a person the entity has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures`
    `the Inspector-General of Taxation`
    `a director, secretary or senior manager of the company`
    `another employee or officer of the company whose functions or duties relate to its tax affairs`
    `a trustee of the trust`
    `a person a trustee has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures`
    `a partner in the partnership`
    `a person a partner has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures`
    `someone else`

-- s 2: "eligible recipient has the meaning given by section 14ZZV". s 14ZZV(1): for every entity, (a) an auditor or
-- audit team member, (b) a registered tax or BAS agent serving the entity, (c) a person the entity authorises, (d) a
-- person or body prescribed -- Regulations s 13A: "the Inspector-General of Taxation is prescribed in relation to all
-- entities". (2) a body corporate's director, secretary, senior manager, or other employee or officer with tax affairs
-- functions; (3) a trust's trustee or a person a trustee authorises; (4) a partnership's partner or a person a partner
-- authorises. A definition handed to a list (ruling of 2026-11-05 (a)): JUST FALSE where no head holds, including a
-- director, trustee or partner of an entity that is not of that kind. The Commissioner and the Tax Practitioners Board
-- are not eligible recipients; a disclosure to them qualifies under s 14ZZT(1) or (1A) instead.
@export Is the person I told (or plan to tell) an "eligible recipient" for the entity, so that a disclosure to them about its tax affairs can be protected (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZV and Taxation Administration Regulations 2017 s 13A)? Auditors, the entity's tax or BAS agent, people it authorises, the Inspector-General of Taxation, and, depending on the entity, a company's directors, secretary, senior managers and tax staff, a trust's trustees, or a partnership's partners.
GIVEN `what kind of entity is it` IS A `Kind of entity` @desc What kind of entity you are reporting on: a body corporate such as a company, a trust, a partnership, or another kind of entity (for example an individual or an association)
      `who did I tell` IS A `The person I told` @desc Whom you told or plan to tell: an auditor or audit team member; the entity's registered tax agent or BAS agent; a person the entity authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures; the Inspector-General of Taxation; for a company, a director, secretary or senior manager, or another employee or officer whose work relates to its tax affairs; for a trust, a trustee or a person a trustee authorised; for a partnership, a partner or a person a partner authorised; or someone else
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the person I told an eligible recipient` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `an auditor of the entity, or a member of an audit team auditing it` THEN TRUE
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a registered tax agent or BAS agent who provides it tax agent or BAS services` THEN TRUE
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a person the entity has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures` THEN TRUE
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `the Inspector-General of Taxation` THEN TRUE
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a director, secretary or senior manager of the company` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a body corporate, such as a company`
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `another employee or officer of the company whose functions or duties relate to its tax affairs` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a body corporate, such as a company`
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a trustee of the trust` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a trust`
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a person a trustee has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a trust`
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a partner in the partnership` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a partnership`
           IF `who did I tell` EQUALS `a person a partner has authorised to receive whistleblower disclosures` THEN `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a partnership`
           OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 14ZZT -- does my disclosure qualify (only if you have reported, or are thinking of reporting, tax misconduct)`

DECLARE `Whom I disclosed to` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner of Taxation`
    `the Tax Practitioners Board`
    `an eligible recipient for the entity`
    `a legal practitioner, for advice or representation about these protections`
    `a medical practitioner or psychologist, for care, treatment or counselling`
    `a body I am a member of, for help about these protections`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Protection route` IS ONE OF
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to the Commissioner (s 14ZZT(1))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to assist the Tax Practitioners Board (s 14ZZT(1A))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to an eligible recipient (s 14ZZT(2))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to a lawyer (s 14ZZT(3))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to a doctor or psychologist (s 14ZZT(3B))`
    `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`

-- s 14ZZT: a disclosure "qualifies for protection under this Part if" -- (1) an eligible whistleblower discloses to the
-- Commissioner and "considers that the information may assist the Commissioner to perform his or her functions or
-- duties under a taxation law in relation to the entity or an associate"; (1A) an eligible whistleblower discloses to
-- the Tax Practitioners Board or the Commissioner and considers that it may assist the Board under the Tax Agent
-- Services Act 2009; (2) an eligible whistleblower discloses to an eligible recipient, "has reasonable grounds to suspect
-- that the information indicates misconduct, or an improper state of affairs or circumstances, in relation to the tax
-- affairs of the entity or an associate", and considers that it may assist the recipient's functions or duties in
-- relation to those tax affairs; (3) anyone discloses to a legal practitioner "for the purpose of obtaining legal advice
-- or legal representation in relation to the operation of this Part"; (3A) to an entity with members that is "prescribed
-- by the regulations" and of which the discloser is a member, for assistance about the Part -- the deposited
-- Regulations prescribe none (finding 2), so it cannot be met; (3B) anyone discloses to a medical practitioner or
-- psychologist "for the purpose of obtaining medical or psychiatric care, treatment or counselling". Each is an "if":
-- a disclosure meeting none of them does not qualify under the Part. Where a disclosure to the Commissioner meets both
-- (1) and (1A), (1) is named. Note to s 14ZZT: the discloser need not give their name.
@export Does my disclosure about an entity's tax affairs qualify for the tax whistleblower protections (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZT)? Disclosures to the ATO (the Commissioner) or the Tax Practitioners Board qualify if you think the information may help them; disclosures to an eligible recipient inside or around the entity qualify if you also have reasonable grounds to suspect tax misconduct; disclosures to a lawyer about these protections, or to a doctor or psychologist for care, always qualify. You need not give your name.
GIVEN `am I an eligible whistleblower` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an eligible whistleblower for the entity (the answer to the eligible whistleblower question)
      `whom did I disclose to` IS A `Whom I disclosed to` @desc To whom you made the disclosure: the Commissioner of Taxation (the ATO); the Tax Practitioners Board; an eligible recipient for the entity (the answer to the eligible recipient question); a lawyer, to get advice or representation about these protections; a doctor or psychologist, to get medical or psychiatric care, treatment or counselling; a union, association or other body you are a member of, to get help about these protections; or someone else
      `do I think it may help the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consider the information may help the Commissioner perform functions or duties under a tax law in relation to the entity or an associate of it
      `do I think it may help the Tax Practitioners Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consider the information may help the Tax Practitioners Board perform its functions under the Tax Agent Services Act 2009 in relation to the entity or an associate of it (for example about a tax agent's conduct)
      `do I have reasonable grounds to suspect tax misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to suspect that the information indicates misconduct, or an improper state of affairs or circumstances, in relation to the tax affairs of the entity or an associate of it
      `do I think it may help the recipient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a disclosure to an eligible recipient: whether you consider the information may help that person perform functions or duties in relation to the tax affairs of the entity or an associate of it
GIVETH A `Protection route`
`Does my disclosure qualify for protection` MEANS
    CONSIDER `whom did I disclose to`
    WHEN `a legal practitioner, for advice or representation about these protections` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to a lawyer (s 14ZZT(3))`
    WHEN `a medical practitioner or psychologist, for care, treatment or counselling` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to a doctor or psychologist (s 14ZZT(3B))`
    WHEN `the Commissioner of Taxation` THEN
        BRANCH IF `am I an eligible whistleblower` AND `do I think it may help the Commissioner` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to the Commissioner (s 14ZZT(1))`
               IF `am I an eligible whistleblower` AND `do I think it may help the Tax Practitioners Board` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to assist the Tax Practitioners Board (s 14ZZT(1A))`
               OTHERWISE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `the Tax Practitioners Board` THEN
        IF `am I an eligible whistleblower` AND `do I think it may help the Tax Practitioners Board`
        THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to assist the Tax Practitioners Board (s 14ZZT(1A))`
        ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `an eligible recipient for the entity` THEN
        IF `am I an eligible whistleblower` AND `do I have reasonable grounds to suspect tax misconduct` AND `do I think it may help the recipient`
        THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to an eligible recipient (s 14ZZT(2))`
        ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    OTHERWISE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`

§§ `ss 14ZZX, 14ZZXA and 14ZZZB -- what protection I have (only if you have made a disclosure)`

GIVEN `the route` IS A `Protection route`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it qualified` `the route` MEANS NOT (`the route` EQUALS `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`)

-- s 14ZZX(1): "If a person makes a disclosure that qualifies for protection under this Part: (a) the person is not
-- subject to any civil, criminal or administrative liability (including disciplinary action) for making the disclosure;
-- and (b) no contractual or other remedy may be enforced, and no contractual or other right may be exercised, against
-- the person on the basis of the disclosure". (2): qualified privilege, and no termination of a contract "on the basis
-- that the disclosure constitutes a breach of the contract". Flat negatives: JUST TRUE. The Note: no immunity for the
-- person's own conduct that the disclosure reveals. For a disclosure that does not qualify the Part is silent (other
-- law may protect it): NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZX reaches only qualifying disclosures).
@export Am I protected from being sued, prosecuted, disciplined, or having a contract enforced or ended against me, for making my disclosure (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZX)? Yes, for a disclosure that qualifies for protection, and you have qualified privilege against defamation; but you are not protected for your own misconduct that the disclosure reveals.
GIVEN `how did my disclosure qualify` IS A `Protection route` @desc How your disclosure qualified for protection, or that it did not (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected from liability for my disclosure` MEANS
    IF `it qualified` `how did my disclosure qualify` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZX reaches only qualifying disclosures)

-- s 14ZZX(1)(c): "if the disclosure was a disclosure of information to the Commissioner or the Tax Practitioners
-- Board--the information is not admissible in evidence against the person in criminal proceedings or in proceedings for
-- the imposition of a penalty, other than proceedings in respect of the falsity of the information." Routes (1) and (1A)
-- are the disclosures to the Commissioner or the Board. A qualifying disclosure to anyone else fails the paragraph's own
-- "if": JUST FALSE (fork F3). Proceedings about falsity are excepted: JUST FALSE (fork F4). A disclosure that does not
-- qualify: NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZX reaches only qualifying disclosures).
@export Is the information I gave the ATO or the Tax Practitioners Board kept out of evidence against me in criminal or penalty proceedings (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZX(1)(c))? Only for a qualifying disclosure to the Commissioner or the Board, and not in proceedings about whether the information was false.
GIVEN `how did my disclosure qualify` IS A `Protection route` @desc How your disclosure qualified for protection, or that it did not (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
      `are the proceedings about whether it was false` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are about the falsity of the information you disclosed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my disclosure kept out of evidence against me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`it qualified` `how did my disclosure qualify`)) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZX reaches only qualifying disclosures)
           IF `how did my disclosure qualify` EQUALS `it qualifies: a disclosure to the Commissioner (s 14ZZT(1))` OR `how did my disclosure qualify` EQUALS `it qualifies: a disclosure to assist the Tax Practitioners Board (s 14ZZT(1A))` THEN `as an answer` (NOT `are the proceedings about whether it was false`)
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Where my claim for protection stands` IS ONE OF
    `the other side must now prove my claim is not made out; the claim is heard separately and the case against me is adjourned until then`
    `I must first point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that my claim is made out; the claim is heard separately and the case against me is adjourned until then`
    `s 14ZZXA does not apply: these are not court proceedings brought against me`

-- s 14ZZXA(1): "If, in civil or criminal proceedings (the primary proceedings) instituted against an individual in a
-- court, the individual makes a claim ... that, because of section 14ZZX, the individual is not subject to any civil,
-- criminal or administrative liability for making a particular disclosure": (a) the individual bears the onus of
-- adducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable possibility that the claim is made out; (b) if discharged,
-- the party instituting the proceedings bears the onus of proving the claim is not made out; (c) separate proceedings;
-- (d) the primary proceedings adjourned; (e) what the individual says or adduces there is not admissible against them
-- (except as to its falsity); (f) no waiver of privilege. An "if": outside court proceedings against the individual, the
-- section does not apply.
@export I am being sued or prosecuted in court and claim the whistleblower immunity: who has to prove what, and what happens to the case against me (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZXA)? You need only point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that your claim is made out; the other side must then prove it is not. The claim is heard in separate proceedings, the case against you waits, and what you say there cannot be used against you.
GIVEN `are these court proceedings against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are civil or criminal proceedings in a court, brought against you
      `have I pointed to evidence of a reasonable possibility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have adduced or pointed to evidence that suggests a reasonable possibility that your disclosure qualified and the immunity applies
GIVETH A `Where my claim for protection stands`
`Who must prove my claim to protection` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `are these court proceedings against me`) THEN `s 14ZZXA does not apply: these are not court proceedings brought against me`
           IF `have I pointed to evidence of a reasonable possibility` THEN `the other side must now prove my claim is not made out; the claim is heard separately and the case against me is adjourned until then`
           OTHERWISE `I must first point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that my claim is made out; the claim is heard separately and the case against me is adjourned until then`

-- s 14ZZZB: the discloser "or any other person is not to be required (a) to disclose to a court or tribunal (i) the
-- identity of the discloser; or (ii) information that is likely to lead to the identification of the discloser; or (b)
-- to produce" a document containing it, "except where: (c) it is necessary to do so for the purposes of giving effect
-- to this Part; or (d) the court or tribunal thinks it necessary in the interests of justice to do so." A flat
-- negative: JUST TRUE. Where an exception is met the shield is lifted: JUST FALSE (fork F5). A disclosure that does not
-- qualify: NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZZB reaches only qualifying disclosures).
@export Can I (or anyone else) be made to reveal my identity as a whistleblower, or documents that would identify me, to a court or tribunal (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZB)? Not for a qualifying disclosure, unless it is necessary to give effect to the whistleblower protections or the court or tribunal thinks it necessary in the interests of justice. Answers TRUE when you are shielded.
GIVEN `how did my disclosure qualify` IS A `Protection route` @desc How your disclosure qualified for protection, or that it did not (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
      `is it necessary to give effect to the protections` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing your identity to the court or tribunal is necessary for the purposes of giving effect to the whistleblower protections (for example in your own claim for compensation)
      `does the court think it necessary in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court or tribunal thinks it necessary in the interests of justice that your identity be revealed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I shielded from revealing my identity to a court or tribunal` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`it qualified` `how did my disclosure qualify`)) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 14ZZZB reaches only qualifying disclosures)
           IF `is it necessary to give effect to the protections` OR `does the court think it necessary in the interests of justice` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 14ZZW and 14ZZY -- offences against me (only if your identity was revealed, or you were harmed or threatened)`

DECLARE `What was revealed` IS ONE OF
    `my identity`
    `information likely to lead to my identification`
    `nothing that identifies me`

DECLARE `Whom it was revealed to` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner`
    `a member of the Australian Federal Police`
    `a lawyer, for advice or representation about these protections`
    `the Inspector-General of Taxation, the Tax Practitioners Board or the ACNC Commissioner`
    `anyone else`

-- s 14ZZW, as a regulative rule from the whistleblower's seat. (1): the first person "commits an offence if" the
-- discloser made a qualifying disclosure and the first person discloses "(i) the identity of the discloser; (ii)
-- information that is likely to lead to the identification of the discloser", obtained "directly or indirectly because
-- of the qualifying disclosure", and "the disclosure ... is not authorised under subsection (2)". The prohibition,
-- written once: the person who learned of the discloser through the disclosure SHANT reveal them in a way that is not
-- permitted. Time is one step: the act asked about happens at 0, and the rule's window closes at 1.
DECLARE `Person who learned of me` IS ONE OF `the person who learned who I am through my disclosure`
DECLARE `Revealing me` IS ONE OF
    `reveal who I am, or what is likely to identify me` HAS `was it permitted` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Person who learned of me` `Revealing me`
`the duty to keep my identity confidential` MEANS
    PARTY `the person who learned who I am through my disclosure`
    SHANT `reveal who I am, or what is likely to identify me` permitted PROVIDED (NOT permitted)
    WITHIN 1
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person who learned who I am through my disclosure` BECAUSE "revealed the identity of a tax whistleblower, or information likely to identify them, without authority (s 14ZZW(1) of the Taxation Administration Act 1953)"

-- The trace helper. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH: a permitted act
-- does not match the prohibited pattern, the window passes, and the trace is FULFILLED; an act not permitted is a
-- breach.
GIVEN `it was permitted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`revealing me broke the duty of confidentiality` `it was permitted` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the duty to keep my identity confidential` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the person who learned who I am through my disclosure` (`reveal who I am, or what is likely to identify me` `it was permitted`) 0), (WAITUNTIL 2))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 14ZZW(2): authorised if made to the Commissioner, an AFP member, a legal practitioner for advice or representation
-- about the Part, a person or body prescribed (Regulations s 13B: the Inspector-General of Taxation, the Tax
-- Practitioners Board, the Commissioner of the ACNC), or "with the consent of the discloser". (3): "Subsection (1) does
-- not apply if" what was revealed "is not of the identity of the discloser", is "reasonably necessary for the purposes
-- of investigating" the matters disclosed, and the first person "takes all reasonable steps to reduce the risk" of
-- identification (an evidential burden on the defendant). "Commits an offence if": JUST FALSE where a condition fails.
@export Did someone commit an offence by revealing my identity as a tax whistleblower, or information likely to identify me (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZW)? It is an offence unless it was told to the Commissioner, the Australian Federal Police, a lawyer advising on these protections, the Inspector-General of Taxation, the Tax Practitioners Board or the ACNC Commissioner, or was with your consent, or (for information other than your identity) was reasonably necessary to investigate and all reasonable steps were taken to protect you.
GIVEN `does my disclosure qualify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your disclosure qualified for protection (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
      `what did they reveal` IS A `What was revealed` @desc What the person revealed: your identity, information likely to lead to your identification, or nothing that identifies you
      `did they get it because of my disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person obtained that information, directly or indirectly, because of your disclosure
      `to whom did they reveal it` IS A `Whom it was revealed to` @desc To whom it was revealed: the Commissioner of Taxation; a member of the Australian Federal Police; a lawyer, for advice or representation about these protections; the Inspector-General of Taxation, the Tax Practitioners Board or the Commissioner of the Australian Charities and Not-for-profits Commission; or anyone else
      `did I consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consented to it being revealed
      `was it reasonably necessary to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing it was reasonably necessary to investigate the misconduct or improper state of affairs your disclosure was about
      `did they take all reasonable steps to protect me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person took all reasonable steps to reduce the risk that you would be identified
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was revealing my identity an offence` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `does my disclosure qualify`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `what did they reveal` EQUALS `nothing that identifies me` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `did they get it because of my disclosure`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the investigation exception applies` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `as an answer` (`revealing me broke the duty of confidentiality` `it was authorised`)
    WHERE
        `it was authorised` MEANS (NOT (`to whom did they reveal it` EQUALS `anyone else`)) OR `did I consent`
        `the investigation exception applies` MEANS
                `what did they reveal` EQUALS `information likely to lead to my identification`
            AND `was it reasonably necessary to investigate`
            AND `did they take all reasonable steps to protect me`

-- s 14ZZY(1): a person "commits an offence if" their conduct causes any detriment to another person and, when they
-- engage in it, they believe or suspect that that person "or any other person made, may have made, proposes to make or
-- could make" a qualifying disclosure, and the belief or suspicion "is the reason, or part of the reason". (2): a person
-- commits an offence if they make a threat to cause detriment to the second person or a third person, intending the
-- second person to fear it will be carried out or reckless as to that, and make the threat "because a person" makes or
-- may make a qualifying disclosure. (3): express or implied, conditional or unconditional; (4): actual fear need not be
-- proved. s 14ZZZAA: detriment "includes (without limitation)" the listed harms. An "if": JUST FALSE where it fails.
@export Did the person who harmed or threatened me commit the offence of victimising a tax whistleblower (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZY)? It is an offence to cause detriment to someone because of a belief or suspicion that they (or anyone) made or might make a qualifying disclosure, or to threaten detriment for that reason intending, or reckless as to, the threatened person's fear.
GIVEN `did they cause me detriment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person's conduct caused you detriment, for example dismissal, injury in your employment, a worse position or duties, discrimination against you among other employees, harassment or intimidation, harm including psychological harm, or damage to your property, reputation, business or finances, or any other damage
      `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For harm caused: whether, when they acted, the person believed or suspected that you or someone else made, may have made, proposed to make or could make a qualifying disclosure
      `did they threaten detriment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person threatened (expressly or by implication, conditionally or not) to cause detriment to you or to someone else
      `did they intend me to fear it, or not care whether I did` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a threat: whether the person intended you to fear the threat would be carried out, or was reckless about whether you would (you need not actually have been afraid)
      `was a disclosure the reason or part of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a qualifying disclosure, made or possible (or for harm caused, the belief or suspicion of one), was the reason or part of the reason for the conduct or threat
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did they commit the victimisation offence` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`the s 14ZZY(1) offence` OR `the s 14ZZY(2) offence`)
    WHERE
        `the s 14ZZY(1) offence` MEANS `did they cause me detriment` AND `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` AND `was a disclosure the reason or part of it`
        `the s 14ZZY(2) offence` MEANS `did they threaten detriment` AND `did they intend me to fear it, or not care whether I did` AND `was a disclosure the reason or part of it`

DECLARE `Part IVD offence` IS ONE OF
    `revealing a whistleblower's identity (s 14ZZW)`
    `causing detriment to a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(1))`
    `threatening a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(2))`

-- The Penalty lines: s 14ZZW(1) "Imprisonment for 6 months or 60 penalty units, or both"; s 14ZZY(1) and (2)
-- "Imprisonment for 2 years or 240 penalty units, or both".
@export What is the longest prison term, in months, for an offence against a tax whistleblower (Taxation Administration Act 1953 ss 14ZZW and 14ZZY)? 6 months for revealing your identity; 2 years for harming or threatening you.
GIVEN `which offence` IS A `Part IVD offence` @desc Which offence: revealing a whistleblower's identity (s 14ZZW), causing detriment to a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(1)), or threatening a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(2))
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The longest prison term in months` MEANS
    IF `which offence` EQUALS `revealing a whistleblower's identity (s 14ZZW)` THEN 6 ELSE 24

GIVEN `which offence` IS A `Part IVD offence`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the penalty units for` `which offence` MEANS
    IF `which offence` EQUALS `revealing a whistleblower's identity (s 14ZZW)` THEN 60 ELSE 240

@export What is the largest fine, in dollars, for an offence against a tax whistleblower (Taxation Administration Act 1953 ss 14ZZW and 14ZZY)? 60 penalty units for revealing your identity; 240 penalty units for harming or threatening you; a fine, prison, or both. (For a company, Crimes Act 1914 s 4B(3) may multiply the fine; not encoded.)
GIVEN `which offence` IS A `Part IVD offence` @desc Which offence: revealing a whistleblower's identity (s 14ZZW), causing detriment to a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(1)), or threatening a whistleblower (s 14ZZY(2))
      `the penalty unit` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of one Commonwealth penalty unit in dollars (Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA: $330, subject to indexation; use the value in force when the offence was committed)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The largest fine in dollars` MEANS `the penalty units for` `which offence` TIMES `the penalty unit`

§§ `ss 14ZZZ to 14ZZZD -- compensation and other remedies (only if you were harmed or threatened)`

DECLARE `Whom I seek the order against` IS ONE OF
    `the person who harmed or threatened me`
    `an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct`
    `a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct`

-- s 14ZZZ(1): "A court may make an order under section 14ZZZA in relation to a person (the first person) if" their
-- conduct causes any detriment to the second person or is a threat to cause it, and when they engage in it they believe
-- or suspect that the second person or any other person "made, may have made, proposes to make or could make" a
-- qualifying disclosure, and that belief or suspicion "is the reason, or part of the reason". (2): an officer or employee
-- of a body corporate whose conduct meets (1), who aided, abetted, counselled or procured, induced, was knowingly
-- concerned in or party to, or conspired in it. (2A): a body corporate under a duty to prevent, or to take reasonable
-- steps against, a third person's detrimental conduct, that fails in part or whole to fulfil it. s 14ZZZD: proceedings
-- may be brought whether or not anyone is or can be prosecuted under s 14ZZY. The court's power is "may ... if"; the
-- answer is whether the court may make an order: JUST FALSE where the "if" fails.
@export Can a court order compensation or other remedies against a person who harmed me, or threatened to, because they believed or suspected I had made or might make a tax whistleblower disclosure (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZ)? Officers and employees involved in a company's conduct, and a company that failed in a duty to prevent another person's conduct, can also be ordered. You can sue whether or not anyone is prosecuted.
GIVEN `whom do I seek the order against` IS A `Whom I seek the order against` @desc Whether you seek the order against the person (or company) that harmed or threatened you, an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct, or a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct
      `was I harmed or threatened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct caused you detriment (for example dismissal, injury, a worse position or duties, discrimination, harassment, psychological harm, damage to your property, reputation, business or finances, or any other damage) or was a threat to cause detriment to you
      `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they acted, the person who harmed or threatened you believed or suspected that you or someone else made, may have made, proposed to make or could make a qualifying disclosure
      `was that belief part of the reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that belief or suspicion was the reason, or part of the reason, for the conduct
      `was the officer or employee involved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an officer or employee: whether they aided, abetted, counselled or procured, induced, were knowingly concerned in or party to, or conspired in the company's conduct
      `did the company have a duty to prevent it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a company with a duty: whether it was under a duty to prevent the other person's conduct, or to take reasonable steps to ensure it did not happen
      `did the company fail in that duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a company with a duty: whether it failed, wholly or partly, to fulfil that duty
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court order a remedy for the harm I suffered` MEANS
    CONSIDER `whom do I seek the order against`
    WHEN `the person who harmed or threatened me` THEN `as an answer` `the s 14ZZZ(1) conditions hold`
    WHEN `an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct` THEN `as an answer` (`the s 14ZZZ(1) conditions hold` AND `was the officer or employee involved`)
    WHEN `a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct` THEN `as an answer` (`the s 14ZZZ(1) conditions hold` AND `did the company have a duty to prevent it` AND `did the company fail in that duty`)
    WHERE
        `the s 14ZZZ(1) conditions hold` MEANS `was I harmed or threatened` AND `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` AND `was that belief part of the reason`

DECLARE `Where the burden of proof stands` IS ONE OF
    `the other side must now prove my claim is not made out`
    `I must first point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of the harm or threat (and, against a company with a duty, of that duty)`

-- s 14ZZZ(2B): "(a) the person seeking the order bears the onus of adducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a
-- reasonable possibility of the matters in" (1)(a) (the detriment or threat), or for (2A) paragraphs (a) and (d) (the
-- conduct and the duty); "(b) if that onus is discharged--the other person bears the onus of proving that the claim is
-- not made out". The belief or suspicion and the reason need not be shown by the claimant first.
@export In my claim for compensation as a tax whistleblower, who has to prove what (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZ(2B))? You need only point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that you were harmed or threatened (and, against a company, that it had a duty to prevent it); the other side must then prove your claim is not made out, including that the disclosure was not part of the reason.
GIVEN `have I pointed to evidence of a reasonable possibility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have adduced or pointed to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that the conduct harmed or threatened you (and, against a company with a duty to prevent it, that it had that duty)
GIVETH A `Where the burden of proof stands`
`Who must prove what in my compensation claim` MEANS
    IF `have I pointed to evidence of a reasonable possibility`
    THEN `the other side must now prove my claim is not made out`
    ELSE `I must first point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of the harm or threat (and, against a company with a duty, of that duty)`

DECLARE `Whistleblower remedy` IS ONE OF
    `compensation from the person for my loss, damage or injury`
    `compensation from the person's employer, in part, jointly and severally with the person, or alone`
    `an injunction to prevent, stop or remedy the effects of the conduct`
    `an apology`
    `reinstatement in my position or one at a comparable level`
    `exemplary damages`
    `any other order the court thinks appropriate`

-- s 14ZZZA(1): the court may order (a) compensation by the first person; (b) "if the court is satisfied that the first
-- person engaged in the detrimental conduct in connection with the first person's position as an employee",
-- compensation by the first person and the employer in parts, jointly (and severally, (4)), or by the employer alone;
-- (c) an injunction; (d) an apology; (e) "if the second person is or was employed in a particular position and the
-- detrimental conduct wholly or partly consists, or consisted, of the termination, or purported termination, of the
-- second person's employment", reinstatement in that position or one at a comparable level; (f) "if the court thinks
-- it is appropriate", exemplary damages; (g) "any other order the court thinks appropriate". (3): for an employer
-- order the court may consider the employer's precautions and due diligence, its whistleblower policy, and its duties.
-- The list of what MAY be ordered; (f) and (g) are left to the court and always listed.
@export What orders can the court make for me against the person who harmed or threatened me for being a tax whistleblower (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZA)?
GIVEN `can the court order a remedy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court may make an order (the answer to the question whether the court can order a remedy for the harm)
      `did they act in their position as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the person engaged in the conduct in connection with their position as an employee (so their employer can be ordered to pay too, or instead)
      `did the conduct end my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was or included the termination, or purported termination, of your employment in a particular position (a resignation you were forced into is asked separately, for the compensation question)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Whistleblower remedy`
`What orders can the court make for me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `can the court order a remedy`)
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE `compensation from the person for my loss, damage or injury` FOLLOWED BY (IF `did they act in their position as an employee` THEN `compensation from the person's employer, in part, jointly and severally with the person, or alone` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` ELSE `the rest`)
    WHERE
        `the tail` MEANS LIST `exemplary damages`, `any other order the court thinks appropriate`
        `the rest` MEANS
            `an injunction to prevent, stop or remedy the effects of the conduct` FOLLOWED BY (`an apology` FOLLOWED BY (IF `did the conduct end my employment` THEN `reinstatement in my position or one at a comparable level` FOLLOWED BY `the tail` ELSE `the tail`))

-- s 14ZZZA(2): "If the detrimental conduct wholly or partly consists, or consisted, of terminating or purporting to
-- terminate a person's employment (including detrimental conduct that forces or forced the person to resign), the court
-- must, in making an order mentioned in paragraph (1)(a) or (b), consider the period, if any, the person is likely to be
-- without employment as a result of the detrimental conduct." An "if": JUST FALSE where it fails. Where no compensation
-- order is being made the subsection is silent: NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 14ZZZA(2), "in making an
-- order mentioned in paragraph (1)(a) or (b)").
@export If the court orders compensation for me, must it take into account how long I am likely to be out of work because I was dismissed or forced to resign (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZA(2))?
GIVEN `is the court making a compensation order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is making (or you are asking for) an order that the person, or their employer, compensate you
      `did the conduct end my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was or included the termination, or purported termination, of your employment
      `did the conduct force me to resign` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct forced you to resign
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the court consider how long I will be out of work` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is the court making a compensation order`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 14ZZZA(2))
    ELSE `as an answer` (`did the conduct end my employment` OR `did the conduct force me to resign`)

-- s 14ZZZC(2): "The claimant must not be ordered by the court to pay costs incurred by another party to the
-- proceedings, except in accordance with subsection (3)". (3): "only if" the court is satisfied the claimant instituted
-- the proceedings "vexatiously or without reasonable cause", or that "the claimant's unreasonable act or omission caused
-- the other party to incur the costs". An "only if": JUST FALSE outside it. (1): any proceeding, including an appeal, in
-- which the claimant seeks a s 14ZZZA(1) order.
@export Can I be ordered to pay the other side's legal costs if I go to court (or appeal) for compensation as a tax whistleblower (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 14ZZZC)? Only if you started the case vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or your unreasonable act or omission caused them the costs.
GIVEN `did I sue vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that you instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `did my unreasonable act cause their costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of yours caused the other party to incur the costs
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to pay the other side's costs` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`did I sue vexatiously or without reasonable cause` OR `did my unreasonable act cause their costs`)
